id: decisions/010-list-order-semantics
title: "When list order means rank"
kind: decision-frame
record_shape: header
status: draft
authored_by: agent-on-behalf
authored_via: "Claude Code session on Dave's behalf, 2026-09-12; drafted from the JUDGMENTS.md reversal axis 'list-order-semantics'; recommendation is an attributed inference and ratifies nothing"
version: "1.0.0"
updated: 2026-09-12
template_ref: templates/decision.yawn
principal_ref: principal:dave

axis: list-order-semantics
axis_gloss: "whether the sequence of items in a list carries rank and is binding, or is presentation with no claim of priority"
poles:
  a: order-is-load-bearing
  b: order-is-display-only
split: genuinely-split
draft_status: full
leverage: 24
leverage_formula: "judgments_resolved(16) x split_weight(1) x authored_conflict_bonus(1.5)"

question: >
  When a record lists things, does the order mean anything, and if only
  sometimes, how does a reader or a bot know which lists are ranked?

why_it_matters: >
  The two written statements say order is not a ladder (scope lenses are
  locations; questions may be visited in any order), while thirteen unlabelled
  lists put attain first among target modes, local first among lanes, confused
  first among sentiments, and the validate chain runs in a fixed order nobody
  calls fixed. A bot that takes the first item as the default inherits a
  priority nobody stated; a bot that ignores order in the tie_break list breaks
  a test.

state_snapshot:
  description: >
    13 judgments sit at order-is-load-bearing (1 authored) and 3 at
    order-is-display-only (2 authored). The repository asserts both directions on
    this axis.
  attributed_to: TAXONOMY.md
  epistemic_status: observed
  source_refs:
    - JUDGMENTS.md
    - TAXONOMY.md

lacuna:
  kind: unselected_choice
  description: >
    Whether Dave intends the validate chain order and the 07:30 radar, 08:20
    readme, 08:40 license, 09:00 daily morning order to be rank; both are
    load-bearing in practice and unstated.

options:
  - name: A
    description: >
      List order is rank. The first entry of any list is the default or highest
      priority unless the record says otherwise.
    cost: "Auditing every enum and holds: list; reordering dave/node.yawn holds to match the conceptual order; writing unordered on lists that are not."
    risk: "attain becomes the default target mode inside the file whose invariants say targets are optional (J0274 against J0219); every alphabetical list becomes a silent ranking."
    reversibility: true
    proof_needed: "A test that compares each enum in schemas/ against its .yawn counterpart as a sequence, not a set."
    evidence_for:
      - "J0199"
      - "J0274"
      - "J0333"
      - "J0344"
      - "J0395"
      - "J0476"
  - name: B
    description: >
      List order carries no meaning. Only a key named priority, precedence, order,
      tie_break or ranked is ordered; every other list is a set and may be sorted
      freely.
    cost: "One sentence in core/yawn-space.yawn or a new core/list-order.yawn; a comment in package.json saying the validate chain is ordered."
    risk: "The validate chain order (J0333) and the morning automation ordering (FACT 17b) are load-bearing in practice and unlabelled; B says they are not."
    reversibility: true
    proof_needed: "Extend tests/state-substrate-v1.test.mjs 'target modes remain exact and do not imply attain' to compare modes as a set; a lint that any key matching priority, precedence, order, tie_break or ranked is a list."
    evidence_for:
      - "J0317"
      - "J0357"
      - "J0676"
  - name: C
    description: >
      List order carries no meaning unless the key is named for rank (priority,
      precedence, order, tie_break, ranked) or the list carries ordered: true with a
      one-line reason. The validate chain in package.json and the automation morning
      schedule are declared ordered with reasons; all other lists are sets.
    cost: "One core sentence; ordered: markers or reasons on the validate chain, the automation cadence, and any enum Dave wants ranked; alphabetize dave/node.yawn holds or mark it."
    risk: "A ranked list that forgets its marker silently becomes a set; the marker must be lint-checked."
    reversibility: true
    proof_needed: "Extend tests/orientation-map-v0.1.test.mjs ('default axis order is a validated partial tie-break and is not expanded') into a repo-wide check: keys named for rank must be lists; unlabelled enums in schemas/ are compared to their .yawn source as sets; scripts/node.yawn declares the validate chain ordered and the test reads package.json to confirm."
    evidence_for:
      - "J0333"
      - "J0357"
      - "J0676"
      - "J0317"

recommendation:
  option_ref: C
  confidence: 0.7
  epistemic_status: inferred
  authority: none
  reasoning: >
    Both authored judgments (J0357, J0676) are display-only. The executable layer
    implements rank exactly where the key says so: core/inquiry-selection.yawn
    names passes 1-2 priority and pass 3 considerations (GROUND_TRUTH FACT 18),
    and the tests enforce the stated tie_break order over the unlabelled
    considerations order (FACT 16). So the practice is already ordered-when-named.
    The thirteen order-is-rank judgments are readings of unlabelled enums that no
    file claims are ranked. C writes the convention down and forces the two
    genuinely ordered unlabelled lists (the validate chain, the morning cadence)
    to say so. It is close because J0333 is proof-attached and unlabelled: the
    repo does depend on an order it never declared.

target_condition:
  mode: decide
  proposed_by: agent-on-behalf
  affected_principals:
    - principal:dave
  ratification_status: proposed
  sufficient_when:
    - "A core file states that list order is rank only when the key is named for rank or carries ordered: true"
    - "scripts/node.yawn or package.json marks the validate chain ordered with a reason, and a test reads it"
    - "Tests compare unlabelled enums (target modes, connection_state, sentiment) as sets, and pass"
    - "dave/node.yawn holds: is either alphabetized by rule or reordered and marked"

choice:
  selected_by: ""
  option_ref: ""
  authority_ref: ""

proof:
  covers:
    - target_condition
    - options
  falsifier: >
    A bot treats the first entry of an unlabelled list as the default (attain as
    the default target mode, local as the default lane) and no record said it was.
  status: proposed

evidence_sample:
  - "J0199"
  - "J0274"
  - "J0333"
  - "J0344"
  - "J0395"
  - "J0476"
  - "J0317"
  - "J0357"
  - "J0676"

boundary:
  - "confidence cannot ratify an objective; this record proposes and Dave decides"
  - "the REVEALED judgments cited here describe what artifacts do, not what Dave chose"

source:
  template: templates/decision.yawn
  axis: TAXONOMY.md
  inventory: JUDGMENTS.md
  id_index: TAXONOMY.md
